### Example usage

```tsx
import CheckoutPage from "./CheckoutPage";
import type { CartItem, DeliveryAddress } from "./types";

const sampleItems: CartItem[] = [
  { id: "1", name: "Wireless Headphones", unitPrice: 8999, quantity: 1 },
  { id: "2", name: "USB-C Cable (2m)", unitPrice: 1299, quantity: 3 },
];

function App() {
  return (
    <CheckoutPage
      items={sampleItems}
      onBackToCart={() => console.log("navigate back to cart")}
      onPay={async (address: DeliveryAddress) => {
        console.log("submitting payment for", address);
        // await api.pay(address)
      }}
    />
  );
}

export default App;
```

Notes on the design:
- `CheckoutPage` is presentational/controlled — it receives `items`, `onBackToCart`, and `onPay` as props, so it stays decoupled from your routing/cart-state/payment implementation.
- Client-side validation covers required fields plus a loose phone format check; wire up real address validation (e.g. postal code per country) if needed.
- The Pay button disables while submitting and when the cart is empty, and surfaces any error thrown by `onPay`.
- Layout is a two-column grid (summary + form) that collapses to one column under 760px.
